WEBVTT

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:04.510
Communication is where intelligence work becomes decision support. A strong product

00:00:04.510 --> 00:00:05.295
separates facts,

00:00:05.295 --> 00:00:09.575
assumptions, analytic judgments, confidence, caveats, and recommended next steps.

00:00:09.575 --> 00:00:13.635
That separation is especially important across jurisdictions because different audiences

00:00:13.635 --> 00:00:15.100
may have different authorities,

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:16.480
risks, and information needs.

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:20.525
The same intelligence may need different versions. Executives may need business impact,

00:00:20.525 --> 00:00:23.445
confidence, recommended action, and decisions required.

00:00:23.445 --> 00:00:26.476
Engineers may need affected systems, indicators, timeframes,

00:00:26.476 --> 00:00:30.830
and operational caveats. Legal and privacy reviewers may need source, authority,

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:33.760
data categories, retention, and dissemination history.

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:37.890
External partners may need a sanitized summary with clear handling markings. Giving every

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:41.620
audience the same raw package can create confusion and unnecessary exposure.

00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:45.505
Confidence language prevents overclaiming. Analysts should say what is observed,

00:00:45.505 --> 00:00:48.710
what is assessed, what is unknown, and what would change the assessment.

00:00:48.710 --> 00:00:52.605
Caveats should be visible: preliminary, limited telemetry, partner-provided,

00:00:52.605 --> 00:00:55.900
not for onward sharing, sanitized, time-bound, or pending review.

00:00:55.900 --> 00:01:00.420
Clear caveats help decision-makers use intelligence without treating it as more certain or

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:01.580
more shareable than it is.

00:01:01.580 --> 00:01:05.790
Escalation paths should be defined before pressure. Urgent risk, privacy concern,

00:01:05.790 --> 00:01:10.265
legal uncertainty, cross-border sharing, public reporting, sensitive partner information,

00:01:10.265 --> 00:01:13.935
potential employee impact, or executive action may require escalation.

00:01:13.935 --> 00:01:17.380
The playbook or operating routine should identify who can approve next steps,

00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:20.370
who provides backup review, and which channel should be used.

00:01:20.370 --> 00:01:23.715
After-action review closes the loop. Teams should ask what worked,

00:01:23.715 --> 00:01:27.670
what slowed coordination, what policy gaps appeared, which data sources were missing,

00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:30.430
whether caveats were understood, whether sharing was appropriate,

00:01:30.430 --> 00:01:31.814
and which records need improvement.

00:01:31.814 --> 00:01:35.530
Reviews should update templates, requirements, partner lists, handling guidance,

00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:37.290
detections, and escalation paths.

00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:41.785
Good communication is precise and modest. It avoids spy-fiction language, speculation,

00:01:41.785 --> 00:01:43.360
and unsupported conclusions.

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:46.795
It respects confidentiality. It tells each audience what they need to know,

00:01:46.795 --> 00:01:49.030
why it matters, how confident the team is,

00:01:49.030 --> 00:01:51.360
and what responsible action is recommended next.
